      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from “nucleus,” reflecting founders’ hope it would be a central hub for surrounding farms and mines.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nucla%2C_Colorado",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Nucla sits at 5,787 feet in the San Miguel Basin, a high plateau that shapes its climate and scenery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.uncovercolorado.com/towns/nucla/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Nearby Cottonwood Cave, a large rock shelter, shows human activity as far back as 270 BC, the earliest corn find in Colorado.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nucla%2C_Colorado",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Tabeguache Pueblo, an early dispersed Ancient Pueblo settlement, was inhabited around AD 1100 before being abandoned.",
